# Native X11 capture buffer reuse

## Scope

The operator approved bounded buffer reuse first, followed by validation.
Unchanged-screen/damage filtering is explicitly out of scope. GPU-resident
true 10-bit capture is a separate future investigation. Preserve continuous
full-frame capture, exact RGB10 identity, x264 and direct HEVC NVENC, private
SHM permissions, and existing frame queues and GPU completion ordering.

Branch: `native10-buffer-reuse`, based on root `6e0c96de` and Linux Host
`8ca14f4e`. Candidate version: `1.4.13-native10-buffer-reuse`. No Client,
macOS, transport, dependency or protocol changes.

## Implementation

The existing twelve-slot image pool remains lazy and bounded. Native10 keeps
already acquired buffers available within a 256 MiB retention floor: eight
3840x2160 images or six 5120x2160 images. It does not allocate that many buffers
ahead or enqueue additional frames. Temporary demand above this floor retains
the existing three-second high-water policy and twelve-slot ceiling. NvFBC
uses the original zero-floor policy.

CUDA-registered images are selected before pageable images, and idle pageable
overflow is retired first. A still-owned pageable overflow image cannot displace
an otherwise reusable registered buffer. Once consumers release the overflow,
the pool returns to its retention floor. The independent, process-wide CUDA pin
budget remains 256 MiB, including images held across capture generations.
Registration failures still use the existing pageable path. x264 images use
the same SHM retention without CUDA registration or GPU roundtrips.

The production pool is extracted into a portable header for behavioral tests.
Moving an existing list node now uses splice, avoiding an allocation on reuse.
Display reinitialization releases pool references and clears usage history;
images still held by an encoder retain their existing shared lifetime.

`native10_pool_acquire` includes allocation, reuse, trimming and backpressure.
`native10_capture_total` now includes that stage; the frame timestamp remains
at actual XShm acquisition, so allocation time is not represented as an older
captured frame. Per-allocation logs report total allocation and CUDA-registration
durations; the completed capture reports allocations, retirements and peak slots.
Dummy encoder initialization no longer updates capture-thread timing arrays
concurrently. Pixel conversion and CUDA synchronization are unchanged.

## Validation

- Normal and ASan/UBSan Native10 fixtures cover exact RGB10 values, geometry
  errors, CUDA registration rollback, byte/count bounds, warm reuse, allocation
  failure, full-pool backpressure, consumer ownership, display replacement and
  pageable overflow/pinned-buffer preference.
- A deterministic twenty-burst fixture reproduces 101 allocations/100 retirements
  with the prior zero-floor policy versus six allocations/no retirements with
  six retained images. This proves allocation behavior, not streaming latency.
- Hosted Linux Host CI runs the sanitizer suite and compiles two hardware-only
  SHM/CUDA probes into its qualification artifact. No GPU probe runs on CI.
- `pool-upload` compares the production policies with real SHM, CUDA registration,
  real four-second gaps and synthetic RGB10 uploads at 3840x2160 and 5120x2160.
  It checks every uploaded bit, owner-only SHM and complete pin-budget release.
  It does not open X11, read/save desktop pixels or change a session.
- The regular `upload` probe checks pinned/pageable uploads independently.
- Full Host build, package, input, audio and transport gates remain mandatory.
  Exact-package installed x264/HEVC streaming, fullscreen playback, display changes
  and reconnect cleanup remain separate acceptance checks. Synthetic pool timings
  must not be presented as a measured frame-rate or GNOME CPU improvement.

Portable and sanitizer fixtures pass; 131 CI policy tests pass. Hardware probes
compile with warnings treated as errors. The qualified Rocky builder produced
standalone probes from exact root `e54e5639` / Host `35a44af8`; the installable
RPM is built separately on GitHub. Initial run `42448939300` restored all four
dependency caches but lacked the newly required GCC 14 ASan/UBSan development
libraries. CI now installs those test-only packages after dependency bootstrap,
without rebuilding or changing the uninstrumented cached dependencies. No product
source change or relaxed sanitizer gate was needed.

The hardware-only comparison passed on the qualified NVIDIA GPU/595.104.02 with
an existing graphical workload active. Four real-time idle/burst cycles per case:

| Frame size | Retention | Allocations / idle retirements | Warm acquire median | Warm acquire p95 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3840x2160 | Previous policy | 29 / 21 | 7.842 ms | 18.622 ms |
| 3840x2160 | Eight buffers | 8 / 0 | 0.002234 ms | 0.014338 ms |
| 5120x2160 | Previous policy | 21 / 15 | 10.146 ms | 16.295 ms |
| 5120x2160 | Six buffers | 6 / 0 | 0.002335 ms | 0.004258 ms |

Every RGB10 upload/readback matched bit-for-bit; SHM permissions and final pin
budget release passed. The separate pinned/pageable upload probe also passed
at both sizes. No X11 capture, service installation or session restart was
performed; the running Host process was preserved and remote scratch removed.
These measurements demonstrate avoided allocation cost under the tested demand
pattern, not a reduction of the complete capture/encode latency by that amount.
They do not qualify installed x264/NVENC streaming. Sanitized binary hashes,
source provenance and detailed measurements are retained under
`artifacts/qualification/native10-buffer-reuse-1.4.13/`.

Hosted integration/RPM gates passed in run `42449585395`, attempt 2, at root
`b57e0796`. Attempt 1 hit the previously observed zero-before-FEC-loss assertion
(174 missing source symbols after exact video recovery); the unchanged retry
passed every gate. No transport code or threshold changed. The 1.4.13 RPM is
verified in the candidate catalog and installed on the authorized hardware Host.
Installed/private-binary and running-supervisor hashes match; administrator
configuration was preserved. The operator confirmed normal playback after reconnect.

Installed Native10/HEVC NVENC at 5120x2160 ran for approximately 19 minutes:
68,450 captured frames, two pool allocations, zero retirements, pool waits or
allocation failures. Mean encoded-packet cadence was 16.6674 ms (approximately
60 fps). Pool acquisition p95 was 0.000741 ms; the maximum 9.44843 ms includes
startup allocation. Six-minute process/GPU sampling saw no additional capture
allocations/reinitializations and stable Host RSS. After reconnect, only the two
current 42.1875 MiB SHM images remain, with no accumulated old segments.

This supports keeping the bounded-reuse change as stall prevention. XShm readback
remains the dominant cost: mean 8.73731 ms, recent-window p95 7.95417 ms in this
session. Earlier baseline samples used a different interval/workload; no sustained
CPU, FPS or total-latency improvement is claimed. x264 has portable buffer/color
coverage but no new installed-session acceptance from this test. Raw operational
records remain private; sanitized values are in the qualification catalog.
